At room temperature, the reaction between water and fluorine produces $\mathrm{F}^{-}, \mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}^{+}$ Fluorine, being non-polar molecule, readily dissolves with water and forms mixture of oxygen and ozone as shown below

\begin{array}{l}
2 \mathrm{F}_{2}+2 \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O} \longrightarrow 4 \mathrm{HF}+\mathrm{O}_{2} \\
3 \mathrm{F}_{2}+3 \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O} \longrightarrow 6 \mathrm{HF}+\mathrm{O}_{3}
\end{array}

HF exist as $\mathrm{H}^{+}$ and $\mathrm{F}^{-}$ in a solution.

$\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{2-}$ and $\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}$ when treated with $\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}$, give white ppt of $BaSO_{3}$ and $BaSO_{4}$ respectively.

\begin{array}{c}
\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}+\mathrm{SO}_{3}^{2-} \longrightarrow \mathrm{BaSO}_{3}+2 \mathrm{Cl}^{-} \\
\text {Barium sulphide} \\
\mathrm{BaCl}_{2}+\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-} \longrightarrow \begin{array}{c}
 \\
\mathrm{BaSO}_{4}+2 \mathrm{Cl}^{-} \\
\text {Barium sulphate }
\end{array}
\end{array}

Out of these $\mathrm{two}, \mathrm{SO}_{3}^{2-}$ is soluble in $\mathrm{HCl}$ but $\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}$ does not.

Ostwald process of for manufacture of nitric acid, $4 \mathrm{NH}_{3}+\mathrm{SO}_{2} \frac{\mathrm{Pt} \text { gauge }}{800-900^{\circ} \mathrm{C}} \underset{\text { nitric oxide }}{4 \mathrm{NO}}+6 \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}$
\Delta H=-21.5 \mathrm{kcal}

$2 \mathrm{NO}+\mathrm{O}_{2} \stackrel{50^{\circ} \mathrm{C}}{\longrightarrow} \quad 2 \mathrm{NO}_{2}4 \mathrm{NO}_{2}+2 \mathrm{H}_{2} \mathrm{O}+\mathrm{O}_{2} \longrightarrow \underset{\text { nitric acid }}{4 \mathrm{HNO}_{3}}$
